WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.694
Welcome to Part 2: plan the job.

2
00:00:03.994 --> 00:00:09.118
By the end, you'll be able to work out the
lights, circuits and amps for any roofline,

3
00:00:09.118 --> 00:00:12.943
and you'll know the limits every
circuit has to stay inside.

4
00:00:14.093 --> 00:00:15.666
First, when you plan.

5
00:00:15.966 --> 00:00:18.300
Have it mapped out before
you're on the ladder.

6
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.096
Do it in the truck on the way,
or on site before you unload.

7
00:00:22.096 --> 00:00:24.094
Never figure it out as you go.

8
00:00:24.394 --> 00:00:26.987
All you need to start
is the feet of each run.

9
00:00:28.137 --> 00:00:30.123
Three numbers do all the work.

10
00:00:30.423 --> 00:00:34.558
1.5. That's lights per
foot, at our 8 inch spacing.

11
00:00:34.858 --> 00:00:39.678
1.37. That's the watts one light
really pulls on Pure White.

12
00:00:39.978 --> 00:00:42.858
And 24. That's our system voltage.

13
00:00:44.008 --> 00:00:45.204
Then four steps.

14
00:00:45.504 --> 00:00:49.504
Step 1. Lights. Feet, times 1.5.

15
00:00:49.804 --> 00:00:55.032
Step 2. Circuits. Lights,
divided by 60. Always round up.

16
00:00:55.332 --> 00:01:01.102
Step 3. Watts per circuit. The
lights on that circuit, times 1.37.

17
00:01:01.402 --> 00:01:05.063
Step 4. Amps per circuit.
Watts, divided by 24.

18
00:01:05.063 --> 00:01:10.532
It has to come out at 4 amps or
less. If it doesn't, add a circuit.

19
00:01:11.682 --> 00:01:15.518
Let's plan a real house. This
one has 150 feet of roofline.

20
00:01:15.518 --> 00:01:20.450
The two edges of the main gable, the
porch, and the front of the garage.

21
00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:27.116
Step 1. 150 feet, times
1.5, is 225 lights.

22
00:01:27.416 --> 00:01:35.135
Step 2. 225 divided by 60 is
3.75. Round up. That's 4 circuits.

23
00:01:35.435 --> 00:01:39.126
So the run splits into 4
groups, about 56 lights each.

24
00:01:39.126 --> 00:01:44.571
Each group gets its own injection point,
and its own feeder back to the box.

25
00:01:44.871 --> 00:01:51.535
Step 3. 56 lights, times
1.37, is about 77 watts.

26
00:01:51.835 --> 00:01:59.652
Step 4. 77 divided by 24 is 3.2 amps.
That's under 4. This plan passes.

27
00:01:59.952 --> 00:02:02.652
Four dots on the house,
about every 37 feet.

28
00:02:02.652 --> 00:02:06.770
That's what you want mapped out
before anyone is on the ladder.

29
00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.962
Every light circuit
stays inside four limits.

30
00:02:11.262 --> 00:02:13.929
60 lights. That's what we aim for.

31
00:02:14.229 --> 00:02:16.994
70 lights. That's the most, ever.

32
00:02:17.294 --> 00:02:21.469
4 amps, and 96 watts.
Those are the most, too.

33
00:02:21.469 --> 00:02:26.257
And they match. 24 volts
times 4 amps is 96 watts.

34
00:02:27.407 --> 00:02:29.634
Always count lights, never feet.

35
00:02:29.934 --> 00:02:33.253
Feet only turn into lights
at our 8 inch spacing.

36
00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:37.906
Change the spacing, and the feet
change. The light count doesn't.

37
00:02:38.206 --> 00:02:44.186
At 8 inch spacing, 60 lights is
40 feet, and pulls about 3.4 amps.

38
00:02:44.486 --> 00:02:48.566
70 lights is 47 feet, and right at 4 amps.

39
00:02:48.866 --> 00:02:54.099
80 lights is 4.6 amps. That's
over. Stop, and add a circuit.

40
00:02:55.249 --> 00:02:58.954
Here's why we use 1.37,
and not the sticker.

41
00:02:59.254 --> 00:03:04.188
The sticker says one light
uses 0.96 watts. On Pure White,

42
00:03:04.188 --> 00:03:11.584
all three colours turn on at once, and it
really pulls 1.37. That's 43 percent more.

43
00:03:11.884 --> 00:03:16.126
Trust the sticker, and you'd
think 100 lights fit on a circuit.

44
00:03:16.126 --> 00:03:17.857
The real number is 70.

45
00:03:18.157 --> 00:03:22.014
That's also why every amp
reading is taken on PW,

46
00:03:22.014 --> 00:03:28.011
the far-left white in the app. Not
BW. BW has blue in it, and pulls less.

47
00:03:29.161 --> 00:03:32.653
One more check before you count
circuits. Look at the box.

48
00:03:32.953 --> 00:03:37.404
The board has 6 circuits. A
single-output controller uses 1,

49
00:03:37.404 --> 00:03:43.068
and leaves 5 for lights. A 4-output
controller uses 5, and leaves just 1.

50
00:03:43.368 --> 00:03:48.089
Then the supply. We use two
sizes, 600 watt and 450 watt,

51
00:03:48.089 --> 00:03:53.805
both at 24 volts. The rules for
each circuit are the same on both.

52
00:03:54.105 --> 00:03:57.741
On a 600 watt box, the
breakers run out first.

53
00:03:57.741 --> 00:04:01.390
Five full circuits is
only about 480 watts.

54
00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:05.321
On a 450 watt box, the
supply can run out first.

55
00:04:05.321 --> 00:04:11.093
Add up the watts of every circuit on
it. The total has to stay under 450.

56
00:04:11.393 --> 00:04:16.761
That's about 328 lights on Pure
White. So on a 450 watt box,

57
00:04:16.761 --> 00:04:20.500
plan 300 or fewer. Five circuits of 60.

58
00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.873
More than the box can take?
It needs a second box.

59
00:04:23.873 --> 00:04:26.826
Tell your crew lead
before anything goes up.

60
00:04:27.976 --> 00:04:29.000
Let's lock it in.

61
00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.634
Have it mapped out before
you're on the ladder.

62
00:04:31.934 --> 00:04:37.402
Lights are feet times 1.5.
Circuits are lights divided by 60,

63
00:04:37.402 --> 00:04:42.492
rounded up. Amps are lights
times 1.37, divided by 24.

64
00:04:42.792 --> 00:04:47.521
Aim for 60 lights. Never
more than 70, or over 4 amps.

65
00:04:47.821 --> 00:04:50.876
Count lights, never feet.
Check the controller,

66
00:04:50.876 --> 00:04:53.595
and on a smaller box, check the total.

67
00:04:53.895 --> 00:04:59.714
Next, take the Part 2 quiz. Part 3
is wire, your kit, and being ready.
